The audio_control component is the audio policy of the Pixel 7. It has no
hardware access. It turns the call state and the requests of the dialer into
the configuration of the audio driver 'pixel_audio' (DDE port of the AoC
sound card and the CS35L41 amplifiers), where an Android device has the audio
HAL with its 'mixer_paths.xml'.

It also writes the configuration of 'record_play_mixer', which carries the
media volume: AoC has no volume command for media playback.

The policy itself is the library 'audio_policy' ('include/audio_policy',
'src/lib/audio_policy'), which is free of Genode sessions and tested on the
host. This component reads the ROMs, calls the library and writes the
results as HID.


Configuration
~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The config holds what is fixed for a scenario. It is read once at start.

! config | amp_mode: asp | asp_gain: 6 | max_media_volume: 0.05
! + mixer | jitter_ms: 10 | record_period_ms: 10
!   + mix left
!     + play | label: waveform_player -> left | volume: media
!   + mix right
!     + play | label: waveform_player -> right | volume: media
!   + mix mic_left  | + play | label: pixel_audio -> mic_left
!   + mix mic_right | + play | label: pixel_audio -> mic_right
!   + policy | label: pixel_audio -> left  | record: left
!   + policy | label: pixel_audio -> right | record: right

:'amp_mode': 'dsp' (default) or 'asp', how the routes that use the
  loudspeakers drive the amplifiers, see "Amplifier mode" below. Any other
  value counts as 'dsp', with a warning.

:'asp_gain': the amplifier gain in mode 'asp', the value of the control 'AMP
  PCM Gain' (0..20 in steps of 1 dB, clamped with a warning; default 6, the
  value of the vendor's 'handset' path). Without effect in mode 'dsp'.

:'max_media_volume': the volume factor of the highest media-volume step
  (0..1, clamped with a warning; default 1.0).

:'mixer': the template of the 'record_play_mixer' config. Without this
  node, no 'mixer_config' report is written. The node is copied to the
  report with all its attributes and sub nodes, at any depth. One thing is
  replaced: an attribute 'volume' with the value 'media' gets the current
  volume factor of media playback. Put it at the 'play' (or 'signal',
  'policy') nodes that carry media; the session labels, period and jitter
  values are those of the scenario.


Inputs
~~~~~~

:ROM 'call_state': the report of 'modem_link' (goa-packages 'telephony').

  ! call_state | state: active | number: +41000000000 | since: 2026-10-01 12:00

  'state' is one of 'unavailable', 'idle', 'incoming', 'outbound',
  'alerting', 'active'. An absent or unknown state counts as 'unavailable'.
  The optional attribute 'band' ('nb' or 'wb', default 'wb') is the band of
  the speech codec. The other attributes are ignored.

:ROM 'audio_request': written by the user interface.

  ! audio_request | speaker: yes | mute: no | volume: 4 | media_volume: 8
  !               | output: speaker | record: no

  'speaker' (default 'no') selects the loudspeaker for a call, 'mute'
  (default 'no') mutes the microphone during a call, 'volume' (0..6,
  default 4, clamped) is the call-volume step. 'media_volume' (0..15,
  default 8, clamped) is the volume step of media playback; it has no
  effect on the config of the driver.

  Two attributes count outside a call only and are ignored during one:
  'output' ('speaker', the default, or 'earpiece'; any other value counts
  as 'speaker', with a warning) is where media plays: on both loudspeakers
  or on the top one alone, driven as in a call on the earpiece. 'record'
  (default 'no') switches the built-in microphones and the capture PCM on,
  for applications that record. Playback and recording do not exclude each
  other.

:ROM 'paths': the path table, generated by 'tool/mixer_paths_to_hid.py' from
  the vendor's 'mixer_paths.xml'. The vendor file and the table generated
  from it are proprietary and never committed.

  ! paths
  ! + defaults
  !   + ctl Main AMP Enable Switch | value: 0
  !   + ctl BUILDIN MIC ID CAPTURE LIST | index: 0 | value: -1
  ! + path voice-handset
  !   + ctl PCM Source | value: ASP
  !   + ctl Main AMP Enable Switch | value: 1
  ! + path hostless-ulC spk-vi
  !   + ctl NoHost1 TX Mixer TDM_0_TX | value: 1
  ! -

  A 'path' node is named after the path and holds its control writes in
  order. A 'ctl' node has the 'name' of the mixer control, a 'value' (an
  integer, a list of integers, or the text of an enum item) and an optional
  'index', the element of a control with several values. Without 'index',
  the write covers the whole control. The 'defaults' node holds the initial
  values, the top-level 'ctl' elements of the XML. If a path name appears
  twice, the first definition counts. The table is limited to 256 paths,
  2048 writes and 64 KiB of names and values; what exceeds the limits is
  dropped with a warning.


Output: driver config
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

The component reports the node 'config' (report label "config"), to be
routed as the 'config' ROM of the driver. Example of a call on the earpiece
in mode 'dsp' (the 'default' nodes shortened to two):

! config | route: handset | voice_band: wb | amp_mode: dsp
! + default Main AMP Enable Switch | value: 0
! + default R Main AMP Enable Switch | value: 0
! + control DSP Booted | value: 0
! + control R DSP Booted | value: 0
! + control DSP1 Preload Switch | value: 1
! + control R DSP1 Preload Switch | value: 1
! + control Boost Peak Current Limit | value: 3.50A
! + control PCM Source | value: ASP
! + control AMP PCM Gain | value: 7
! + control Main AMP Enable Switch | value: 1
! + control NoHost1 TX Mixer TDM_0_TX | value: 1
! + stream | pcm: 15 | direction: capture
! + control BUILDIN MIC ID CAPTURE LIST | index: 0 | value: 0
! + control Audio DSP Mode | value: Telephony
! + control Sidetone Enable | value: 1
! + control EP4 TX Mixer INTERNAL_MIC_TX | value: 1
! + control TDM_0_RX Mixer EP5 | value: 1
! + control Incall Mic Mute | value: 0
! + control Incall Sink Mute | value: 0
! + stream | pcm: 4 | direction: playback
! + stream | pcm: 11 | direction: capture
! + tuning | block: 19 | component: 3 | key: 18 | value: 4
! + playback | pcm: 1 | enabled: no
! + capture | pcm: 8 | enabled: no
! -

The sub nodes form one ordered list:

:'default': 'name', optional 'index', 'value'. The initial value of a mixer
  control, taken from the 'defaults' of the path table. It is the value the
  control has while no 'control' node sets it. The list of 'default' nodes
  is the same in every config and comes first.

:'control': 'name', optional 'index', 'value'. A write of a mixer control.
  Without 'index' the value is meant for the whole control; with 'index'
  for that element, and adjacent nodes of one control form one write.

:'stream': 'pcm', 'direction' ('playback' or 'capture'). An ALSA PCM device
  the driver opens and prepares without transferring data (the hostless and
  voice-call PCMs).

:'tuning': 'block', 'component', 'key', 'value'. A parameter for the tuning
  service of AoC. The only one so far is the call volume (block 19,
  component 3, key 18, value 0..6).

:'playback', 'capture': 'pcm', 'enabled' ('yes' or 'no'). The PCM devices
  that carry the data of the driver's Record and Play sessions.

:'missing': named after a path of the current route that the path table
  does not hold. The writes of that path are absent, the rest of the config
  is complete. The component logs a warning once per name.

The top-level attribute 'route' names the state ('media', 'handset',
'speaker'). In the media route, 'output' ('speaker' or 'earpiece') and
'record' ('yes' or 'no') repeat the two requests that shape it:

! config | route: media | output: earpiece | record: yes | amp_mode: asp

'voice_band' ('nb' or 'wb') is present in a call and is, for
now, only a record of the codec band: the tuning data that depends on it is
not available yet. 'amp_mode' ('dsp' or 'asp') repeats the configured
amplifier mode, so that a log of the config shows which one is active.

A config is always complete for the current inputs, never a difference to
the previous one, and the same inputs give the same bytes. The component
writes a new config only when an input change affects it.

What the driver is expected to do with it: write the 'default' values once
and whenever a control is no longer named by a 'control' node, apply the
'control' nodes in the listed order, open the 'stream' PCMs after their
controls, and close streams before it reverts controls. If two 'control'
nodes name the same control, the later one counts.


Output: mixer config
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

With a 'mixer' node in its config, the component reports a second node
'config' with the report label "mixer_config", to be routed as the 'config'
ROM of 'record_play_mixer'. It is the 'mixer' node of the config with the
volume factor filled in. For the configuration above, with the request
'media_volume: 15' and no call:

! config | jitter_ms: 10 | record_period_ms: 10
! + mix left
!   + play | label: waveform_player -> left | volume: 0.050000
! + mix right
!   + play | label: waveform_player -> right | volume: 0.050000
! + mix mic_left
!   + play | label: pixel_audio -> mic_left
! + mix mic_right
!   + play | label: pixel_audio -> mic_right
! + policy | label: pixel_audio -> left | record: left
! + policy | label: pixel_audio -> right | record: right
! -

The microphone is a Play client of the mixer (the driver's sessions
'mic_left' and 'mic_right'), with mixes of its own. A component that records
gets them through a 'policy' node of the template, and what it plays goes
into the playback mixes like any other media:

! + mix left
!   + play | label: recorder -> left | volume: media
! + policy | label: recorder -> left | record: mic_left

The driver delivers microphone samples only while the request says 'record:
yes'; without it, the capture PCM is closed and the microphone mixes are
silent.

The volume factor of the media-volume step v (0..15) is

! max_media_volume * (v / 15)^2

that is 0 for step 0, 'max_media_volume' for step 15 and a quadratic curve
in between (step 8, the default: 0.284 of the maximum). While a call route
is active (handset, speaker), the factor is 0: media is muted in the mixer
in addition to the disabled playback PCM. The factor is written with up to
six decimals, cut off, never rounded up.

The report is written at start and whenever the factor changes. A request
that changes only the media volume does not touch the driver config.


Policy
~~~~~~

The order of the actions is the one observed from the LineageOS audio HAL.

:media: (state 'unavailable', 'idle' or 'incoming')
  'Audio DSP Mode' = 'Ambient', the path of the output, path
  'low-latency-playbackP', playback PCM 1 enabled. The call requests and
  the band have no effect.

  Output 'speaker': path 'speaker', both amplifiers.

  Output 'earpiece': path 'handset' instead, which sets 'PCM Source' =
  'ASP', 'AMP PCM Gain' = 6 and 'Main AMP Enable Switch' = 1 of the top
  amplifier. Nothing in this config switches the bottom amplifier on: it is
  off through its initial value 'R Main AMP Enable Switch' = 0 (a 'default'
  node), as in a call on the earpiece.

  With 'record: yes', after everything of the output and in front of the
  PCM nodes: paths 'handset-mic' (microphone list, DC blocker, spatial
  module), 'audio-recordC' ('EP1 TX Mixer INTERNAL_MIC_TX', the microphones
  to PCM 8) and 'handset-mic-post' ('MIC Record Soft Gain (dB)' = 22; the
  initial value is -40), and the capture PCM 8 enabled. This is the order
  of a call: device path, use-case path, '-post' path. 'handset-mic' is the
  path of the HAL's built-in microphone back ends
  (seen in a log of LineageOS). The DSP mode
  stays 'Ambient'; whether Android selects 'Record' for a recording was
  never captured.

:handset: (state 'outbound', 'alerting' or 'active', speaker 'no')
  path 'voice-handset', path 'hostless-ulC spk-vi' and stream 15, path
  'voice-handset-mic', 'Audio DSP Mode' = 'Telephony', paths
  'sidetone-for handset', 'voice-callC', 'voice-handset-mic-post',
  'voice-callP', 'Incall Mic Mute' = 0 or 1 (the mute request),
  'Incall Sink Mute' = 0, streams 4 and 11, the call volume, playback PCM 1
  disabled. Only the top amplifier is switched on: 'voice-handset' does not
  name the bottom one, which therefore stays at its initial value 0.

:speaker: (a call with speaker 'yes')
  as handset with the paths 'voice-speaker', 'voice-speaker-mic' and
  'voice-speaker-mic-post', and without the sidetone.

The capture PCM 8 is disabled during a call, whatever the request says: the
microphones belong to the call then (PCM 11). The 'output' request is
ignored as well, 'speaker' decides between earpiece and loudspeaker.


Amplifier mode
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

In the vendor's table the initial value of 'PCM Source' is 'DSP' for both
amplifiers, and the loudspeaker paths 'speaker' and 'voice-speaker' do not
change it: sound passes the amplifier's DSP, which needs its firmware (the
speaker protection). Android's audio HAL loads it once at its start with
the paths 'cs35l41-load-protection-firmware-start' and '-end' (seen in a
log of LineageOS). Without the
firmware, these routes are silent.

:dsp: (default, the vendor's way) Every config, in every route, has the two
  firmware paths between the 'default' nodes and the actions of the route:

  ! + control DSP Booted | value: 0
  ! + control R DSP Booted | value: 0
  ! + control DSP1 Preload Switch | value: 1
  ! + control R DSP1 Preload Switch | value: 1

  The two paths are merged: a write of the first path that the second path
  overrides is left out ('DSP1 Preload Switch' = 0 of the start path), so
  each control appears once, with its final value, and the list is the same
  in every config. (Android's audio_route has the same effect at HAL start:
  it writes a control only when the value changes, so of the two paths only
  the preload switch 0 -> 1 reaches the kernel.) The driver writes it with the first config - the kernel
  then loads 'cs35l41-dsp1-spk-prot.wmfw' and the tuning file of each
  amplifier - and skips it in all later configs because nothing changed:
  the firmware is loaded once and stays loaded. With the paths applied one
  after the other, the driver would see the preload switch change to 0 and
  back to 1 in every config and would reload the firmware at every route
  change, mute and volume step.

  If a firmware file is missing in the scenario, the load fails and the
  loudspeaker routes stay silent, which is the safe failure; the handset
  route does not depend on the firmware. If one of the two paths is not in
  the table, the other one is applied as it is and the missing one is
  reported as 'missing'.

  Known gap: Android's HAL also writes the stored speaker calibration of
  the individual device (coil resistance 'cal_r', ambient temperature
  'cal_ambient', one pair per amplifier) after loading the firmware. This
  component does not, so the protection runs on the nominal values of the
  tuning file.

:asp: No firmware paths. The routes that use the loudspeakers (media,
  speaker) get four more writes after the last path of the route (before
  the playback PCM, or before the in-call mutes and the voice PCMs), so
  that they count if a path names the same controls:

  ! + control AMP PCM Gain | value: 6
  ! + control R AMP PCM Gain | value: 6
  ! + control PCM Source | value: ASP
  ! + control R PCM Source | value: ASP

  The gain is the 'asp_gain' of the config. It comes before the source:
  the driver takes the writes of a route back in reverse order, so an
  amplifier returns to the (silent) DSP source before its gain returns to
  the initial value. THERE IS NO SPEAKER PROTECTION in this mode; it is
  meant for bring-up at a low media volume.

  The routes at the ear (a call on the handset, media on the earpiece) get
  the two source writes only, both amplifiers, after their paths:

  ! + control PCM Source | value: ASP
  ! + control R PCM Source | value: ASP

  The gain of the top amplifier is the one of the path ('voice-handset',
  'handset'; 'asp_gain' does not apply at the ear). The bottom amplifier
  stays off by its initial value 'R Main AMP Enable Switch' = 0. Its source
  is named nevertheless: every config of this mode names both sources, so
  the driver never sets one back to 'DSP' - the codec would then ask for the
  amplifier firmware, which an image of this mode does not have (seen on
  the device: the driver stopped at that request when the route changed
  from the loudspeakers to the earpiece).

In mode 'dsp' the handset route and the earpiece output are as the vendor's
table says: 'voice-handset' and 'handset' select 'PCM Source' = 'ASP' and
their gain for the top amplifier themselves, after the firmware paths, and
the firmware stays loaded in both amplifiers.


Path table tool
~~~~~~~~~~~~~~~

! tool/mixer_paths_to_hid.py <mixer_paths.xml> -o <paths>

The tool follows the parser of Android's 'audio_route.c' (nested paths,
repeated names, the 'id' attribute); its docstring describes the rules and
what is left to the driver.


Tests
~~~~~

! make -C repos/pixel/src/lib/audio_policy/test
! python3 repos/pixel/tool/test_mixer_paths_to_hid.py

On the device: 'run/phone_pixel' of 'repos/muesli', a call at the ear.
